<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tercio</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The tercio was a Spanish infantry formation of the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries that combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">pikemen</a>, swordsmen and arquebusiers in large, disciplined squares. It made the armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> among the strongest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> note that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> reformed this model by breaking the tercio's massive squares into smaller, more manoeuvrable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeers</a> and pikemen.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tercio.jpg" alt="Tercio" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ningyou. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cross_of_Burgundy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The tercio served the Spanish Army under the Catholic Monarchs and Habsburg Spain. Its organisation and tactics grew out of the innovations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-fernandez-de-cordoba">Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba</a>, called El Gran Capitán, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada-war">Granada War</a> (1482–1491) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>, which united the pike with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arquebus">arquebus</a>. The term at first denoted not a combat unit but an administrative one, commanding garrisons in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The first official tercio, that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, was created in 1534, and the tercios of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> followed in 1536.</p><p>The origin of the word is doubtful. Some historians link it to a Roman legion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, or to the threefold division of the Spanish forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, or to three types of fighters. Others derive it from the three thousand men mustered in the first units.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>A tercio was commanded by a maestre de campo appointed by the king. Each originally had ten companies of 300 men, eight of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">pikemen</a> and two of arquebusiers; the companies were later cut to 250 and the share of gunmen rose steadily. The core were Spanish subjects admired for discipline, but the army was supplemented by troops of many nations: in 1621 only seven of its 47 units were of Spanish origin.</p><p>In battle the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">pikemen</a> formed a hollow square holding swordsmen, with groups of arquebusiers at the corners. Tercios fought at Bicocca in 1522, Pavia in 1525, and Mühlberg in 1547, where they helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> defeat a league of Protestant princes. When pay ran out they could mutiny: in April 1576 unpaid tercios occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> and sacked it for three days.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The first real challenge on the open battlefield came at Nieuwpoort in 1600, where Maurice of Orange's shallower linear formations brought more guns to bear. In the Thirty Years' War Gustavus Adolphus defeated tercio-style formations, though the tercios played a decisive part in defeating the Swedish army at Nördlingen in 1634. Their reputation of invincibility in open battle was shattered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rocroi">Rocroi</a> in 1643. In 1704 the Spanish tercios were turned into regiments and the pikeman was dropped. From 1920 the units of the Spanish Legion bore the name tercio as an honorary title.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tercio” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the Habsburgs Castilian infantry formed the core of the <b>tercios</b> that fought in Italy and Flanders, while Castilian conquistadors such as Hernán Cortés and Francisco Pizarro overthrew the Aztec and Inca empires.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Spain’s infantry, organised from the 1530s in the famous <b>tercios</b> of pikemen and musketeers, was regarded as the best in Europe for more than a century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Spanish infantry, victorious at Pavia (1525) and organised from 1534 into <b>tercios</b> combining pikemen and arquebusiers, gave Spain military dominance over its European opponents for more than a century, a reputation that was badly dented by defeat at Rocroi in 1643.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Building on Dutch practice, Gustavus replaced the massive infantry squares of the Spanish <b>tercio</b> with smaller, more manoeuvrable brigades of musketeers and pikemen; introduced light regimental guns that could be moved and fired on the battlefield rather than used mainly for sieges; and employed cavalry aggressively in shock…</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The defeat led to Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba's creation of the <b>tercios</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-28">28 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The defeat of the famed <b>tercios</b> signalled the decline of Spanish power on land.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-19">19 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
